WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.380 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Imagine a robot turning at a crossroads.

2
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.170 line:1% position:50% size:90%
It makes a quarter turn, then a half turn.

3
00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:09.240 line:1% position:50% size:90%
How could we describe each turn
accurately?

4
00:00:10.236 --> 00:00:14.726 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Today we are learning to recognise and
compare angles as turns.

5
00:00:15.739 --> 00:00:20.069 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Our learning goal is to recognise and
compare angles as turns.

6
00:00:21.083 --> 00:00:25.823 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Success criteria: identify vertices and
arms, classify angles

7
00:00:25.823 --> 00:00:27.253 line:1% position:50% size:90%
and compare degrees.

8
00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:33.237 line:1% position:50% size:90%
An angle is the amount of turning between
two rays meeting at a vertex.

9
00:00:33.257 --> 00:00:36.417 line:1% position:50% size:90%
The length of the rays does not change the
angle.

10
00:00:37.468 --> 00:00:42.118 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A full turn is three hundred and sixty
degrees, a half turn is one hundred

11
00:00:42.118 --> 00:00:44.588 line:1% position:50% size:90%
and eighty, and a quarter turn is ninety.

12
00:00:45.573 --> 00:00:47.133 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Look at this angle.

13
00:00:47.183 --> 00:00:50.123 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Is it acute, right, obtuse or straight?

14
00:00:50.173 --> 00:00:52.763 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Pause for five seconds and decide.

15
00:00:57.754 --> 00:01:02.804 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Sixty degrees is smaller than ninety
degrees, so this is an acute angle.

16
00:01:03.842 --> 00:01:06.502 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A right angle is ninety degrees.

17
00:01:06.522 --> 00:01:08.642 line:1% position:50% size:90%
It looks like the corner of a square.

18
00:01:09.718 --> 00:01:13.198 line:1% position:50% size:90%
An angle is one hundred and twenty
degrees.

19
00:01:13.208 --> 00:01:15.518 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Is it acute or obtuse?

20
00:01:15.518 --> 00:01:17.828 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Compare it with ninety degrees.

21
00:01:22.841 --> 00:01:27.181 line:1% position:50% size:90%
One hundred and twenty is greater than
ninety and less than one hundred and

22
00:01:27.181 --> 00:01:29.481 line:1% position:50% size:90%
eighty, so the angle is obtuse.

23
00:01:30.523 --> 00:01:34.603 line:1% position:50% size:90%
An angle with longer arms is not
necessarily larger.

24
00:01:34.663 --> 00:01:38.373 line:1% position:50% size:90%
We measure the amount of turning, not the
length of the lines.

25
00:01:39.378 --> 00:01:43.478 line:1% position:50% size:90%
When measuring with a protractor, place
the centre at the vertex and align

26
00:01:43.478 --> 00:01:46.118 line:1% position:50% size:90%
one arm with the zero-degree baseline.

27
00:01:47.144 --> 00:01:51.204 line:1% position:50% size:90%
If the angle looks narrower than a right
angle, should you read seventy-

28
00:01:51.204 --> 00:01:54.114 line:1% position:50% size:90%
five or one hundred and five degrees?

29
00:01:59.121 --> 00:02:03.541 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Seventy-five degrees is the sensible
measurement because it is less than

30
00:02:03.541 --> 00:02:04.781 line:1% position:50% size:90%
ninety degrees.

31
00:02:05.812 --> 00:02:10.752 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Always check which protractor scale begins
at zero along your starting arm.

32
00:02:10.802 --> 00:02:13.712 line:1% position:50% size:90%
The two scales count in opposite
directions.

33
00:02:14.728 --> 00:02:17.938 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Two adjacent angles lie on a straight
line.

34
00:02:17.978 --> 00:02:19.928 line:1% position:50% size:90%
One is forty degrees.

35
00:02:19.958 --> 00:02:21.758 line:1% position:50% size:90%
What is the other angle?

36
00:02:26.862 --> 00:02:30.442 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A straight angle is one hundred and eighty
degrees.

37
00:02:30.452 --> 00:02:34.262 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Subtract forty to get one hundred and
forty degrees.

38
00:02:35.279 --> 00:02:39.579 line:1% position:50% size:90%
To draw an angle, draw the first ray, mark
the required number of degrees

39
00:02:39.579 --> 00:02:42.349 line:1% position:50% size:90%
from zero and connect the mark to the
vertex.

40
00:02:43.357 --> 00:02:47.007 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A robot makes two quarter turns in the
same direction.

41
00:02:47.047 --> 00:02:50.147 line:1% position:50% size:90%
How many degrees has it turned altogether?

42
00:02:55.196 --> 00:02:59.776 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Ninety plus ninety equals one hundred and
eighty degrees, which is a half

43
00:02:59.776 --> 00:03:00.406 line:1% position:50% size:90%
turn.

44
00:03:01.422 --> 00:03:04.772 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Angles are measured in degrees, not
centimetres.

45
00:03:04.802 --> 00:03:09.292 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A small drawing and a large drawing can
show exactly the same turn.

46
00:03:10.309 --> 00:03:12.389 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Imagine a door opening.

47
00:03:12.419 --> 00:03:17.169 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A small opening makes an acute angle,
while a door opened square to the wall

48
00:03:17.169 --> 00:03:18.519 line:1% position:50% size:90%
makes a right angle.

49
00:03:19.578 --> 00:03:23.678 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A turn of thirty degrees is one third of a
right angle.

50
00:03:23.728 --> 00:03:26.838 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Three such turns add up to ninety degrees.

51
00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:32.290 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A turn of one hundred and fifty degrees is
close to a straight angle, but

52
00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:35.040 line:1% position:50% size:90%
thirty degrees short of one hundred and
eighty.

53
00:03:36.024 --> 00:03:38.174 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Use benchmarks to estimate.

54
00:03:38.184 --> 00:03:42.714 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Compare the angle with ninety degrees
before reading a protractor scale.

55
00:03:43.775 --> 00:03:47.715 line:1% position:50% size:90%
If your measurement says forty degrees but
the drawing looks wider than a

56
00:03:47.715 --> 00:03:50.625 line:1% position:50% size:90%
right angle, check whether you used the
wrong scale.

57
00:03:51.682 --> 00:03:55.942 line:1% position:50% size:90%
When drawing an angle, keep the ruler
steady and make sure both rays begin

58
00:03:55.942 --> 00:03:57.372 line:1% position:50% size:90%
at the same vertex.

59
00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:01.620 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Explain your answer using the size of the
turn.

60
00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.400 line:1% position:50% size:90%
This is stronger than simply saying the
angle looks big.

61
00:04:06.449 --> 00:04:09.509 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Imagine a compass needle pointing north.

62
00:04:09.509 --> 00:04:13.899 line:1% position:50% size:90%
When it rotates to east, it has completed
a quarter turn, even if the needle

63
00:04:13.899 --> 00:04:15.709 line:1% position:50% size:90%
itself is very short.

64
00:04:16.729 --> 00:04:21.009 line:1% position:50% size:90%
If the compass turns from north to south,
it has completed a half turn.

65
00:04:21.029 --> 00:04:24.849 line:1% position:50% size:90%
The direction changed by one hundred and
eighty degrees.

66
00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:29.600 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A turn of forty-five degrees is half of a
right angle.

67
00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:33.950 line:1% position:50% size:90%
We can compare it with the corner of a
square to estimate its size.

68
00:04:34.951 --> 00:04:39.501 line:1% position:50% size:90%
An angle of eighty degrees is acute even
though it is almost a right

69
00:04:39.501 --> 00:04:43.201 line:1% position:50% size:90%
angle. The exact boundary is ninety
degrees.

70
00:04:44.214 --> 00:04:48.914 line:1% position:50% size:90%
An angle of ninety-one degrees is obtuse,
even though it looks very close

71
00:04:48.914 --> 00:04:50.064 line:1% position:50% size:90%
to a right angle.

72
00:04:50.154 --> 00:04:53.584 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Mathematical categories use precise
boundaries.

73
00:04:54.583 --> 00:04:57.503 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Try drawing two rays with a narrow
opening.

74
00:04:57.523 --> 00:05:00.753 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Then rotate one ray without changing its
length.

75
00:05:00.793 --> 00:05:03.003 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Notice how the angle grows.

76
00:05:04.003 --> 00:05:06.823 line:1% position:50% size:90%
The vertex is the common endpoint.

77
00:05:06.853 --> 00:05:11.633 line:1% position:50% size:90%
If the rays do not meet at the same point,
we cannot identify the angle

78
00:05:11.633 --> 00:05:12.833 line:1% position:50% size:90%
in the usual way.

79
00:05:13.858 --> 00:05:18.448 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Imagine a skateboarder changing direction
by one hundred and thirty-five

80
00:05:18.448 --> 00:05:22.938 line:1% position:50% size:90%
degrees. Explain why this turn is obtuse
rather than straight.

81
00:05:23.964 --> 00:05:25.874 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Explain the steps to someone else:

82
00:05:25.874 --> 00:05:30.284 line:1% position:50% size:90%
estimate the angle, align the baseline,
read the correct scale and check the

83
00:05:30.284 --> 00:05:31.194 line:1% position:50% size:90%
angle type.

84
00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:36.750 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Your challenge is to find three angles in
your classroom, classify them and

85
00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:38.180 line:1% position:50% size:90%
explain your reasoning.
